1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,600
 Hey everyone, this is Dr. Marcon and this is chapter 20 on blood vessels.

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:09,080
 So what I'm going to do is I'm actually going to break up blood vessels into regions.

3
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:13,500
 I'm going to talk about the general characteristics of blood vessels and then for this unit I'm

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:22,640
 going to be talking about the blood supply to the head, neck, upper limb as well as the

5
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:24,840
 thoracic region.

6
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:30,160
 And then the next unit will talk about the blood supply to the abdomen as well as the

7
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:34,500
 lower limb.

8
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:38,800
 So we have three types of blood vessels that transport blood in the body.

9
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:43,640
 We have arteries, arteries, capillaries, and veins.

10
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:48,360
 So arteries carry blood away from the heart.

11
00:00:48,360 --> 00:00:50,420
 And then we have arterioles.

12
00:00:50,420 --> 00:00:53,020
 These are the smallest type of arteries.

13
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:54,280
 We have capillaries.

14
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:57,460
 These are the smallest blood vessels.

15
00:00:57,460 --> 00:01:00,400
 And from the capillaries we'll have venules.

16
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:05,760
 Venules will receive blood from capillaries and then the veins will receive blood from

17
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:14,360
 the venules and then these veins will transport blood back towards the heart.

18
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:19,080
 General structure of blood vessels is composed of three layers or tunics.

19
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:22,160
 The inner most layer is the tunica intima.

20
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:26,840
 The tunica intima is the inner most tunic contains endothelium, which is made up of a

21
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:29,800
 simple squamous epithelium.

22
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:36,400
 And usually vessels that are greater than 1 millimeter will also have a sub endothelial

23
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:46,120
 layer, so sub meaning below, so this is a layer below the endothelium.

24
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:51,760
 The second layer, this middle layer is the tunica media.

25
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:57,120
 When you think tunica media, I think middle, muscular layer, the tunica media is made up

26
00:01:57,120 --> 00:01:59,960
 of sheets of smooth muscle.

27
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:04,200
 Contraction of this muscle causes vasoconstriction.

28
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:08,200
 Relaxation of this layer causes vasodilation.

29
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:16,960
 Remember when we talked about what smooth muscle does and how it lines blood vessels

30
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:20,520
 as well as the hollow organs of the body.

31
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:27,080
 The outer most layer is the tunica externa, also known as the tunica adventisia.

32
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:30,520
 This layer is composed of connective tissue.

33
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:34,080
 And finally, we have a centralized space called the lumen.

34
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:42,420
 The lumen is a central blood-filled space of a blood vessel.

35
00:02:42,420 --> 00:02:47,680
 And here we see the generalized structures of arteries, veins, and capillaries.

36
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:52,040
 So arteries, again, being the blood vessels that carry blood away from the heart.

37
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:53,360
 We see the three layers.

38
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:57,480
 We see the tunica intima with the inner most layer, the endothelium.

39
00:02:57,480 --> 00:03:01,360
 Below that layer is that sub endothelial layer.

40
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:05,440
 And then around that would be the internal elastic membrane.

41
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:10,920
 The tunica media is made up of smooth muscle as well as elastic fibers that we talked about

42
00:03:10,920 --> 00:03:13,160
 in unit one.

43
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:15,160
 There is an external elastic membrane.

44
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:22,900
 And then finally, an outer most tunica externa or tunica adventisia made up of collagen fibers.

45
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:31,080
 We then have a blood supply or the vasovasorium layer of these blood vessels.

46
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:34,200
 And of course, there is that centralized space called the lumen.

47
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:38,760
 Now both arteries and veins do have the same generalized structure.

48
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:43,560
 However, there are distinct differences between arteries and veins, which we'll get into.

49
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:51,400
 One of the most distinctive differences, of course, is the thickness of that tunica media,

50
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:52,600
 that middle muscular layer.

51
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:56,800
 We know that arteries have a thicker tunica media.

52
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:03,160
 Also another main difference is that some veins do have valves within their lumen.

53
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:08,400
 And these valves help prevent the back flow of blood.

54
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:14,800
 So then we get down to the capillaries, the capillaries being the smallest vessels.

55
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:20,080
 And they're usually made up of one layer of endothelium.

56
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:28,080
 And they're very porous, meaning they allow for cells to go through and also allow for

57
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:31,560
 gas exchange.

58
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:38,480
 So here we see arteries usually carrying oxygenated blood away from the heart to the rest of the

59
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:43,920
 system and then down to the capillary level where we'll have gas exchange.

60
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:52,760
 And then deoxygenated blood goes into the vaniels and then into the veins to carry deoxygenated

61
00:04:52,760 --> 00:05:01,200
 blood or carbon dioxide rich blood back to the heart.

62
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:07,200
 Here we see a slide of a typical artery and vein.

63
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:13,400
 Again, we can see that distinctive tunica media, that very thick tunica media within an artery,

64
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:18,000
 a lot thicker compared to the thinner walled vein.

65
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,160
 And if you get to actually handle cadaver sections, you'll feel that arteries are a lot

66
00:05:24,160 --> 00:05:32,400
 springier, a lot kind of thicker compared to the thinner walled veins.

67
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:34,560
 Now we have different types of arteries.

68
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:40,440
 The largest arteries are called elastic arteries and they range in diameter from 2.5 centimeter

69
00:05:40,440 --> 00:05:42,560
 to 1 centimeter.

70
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:43,800
 This includes the aorta.

71
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:50,640
 We know the air is one of the largest arteries that carries blood away from the heart.

72
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:56,000
 So the aorta and its major branches are examples of elastic arteries.

73
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,640
 These are sometimes called conducting arteries.

74
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:03,640
 And these types of arteries have a very high elastin content.

75
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:10,480
 So a high amount of elastic connected tissue that helps dampen that surge of blood pressure

76
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:16,800
 and kind of gives the artery that recoil characteristic to allow for the pumping of

77
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:20,600
 blood to the rest of the system.

78
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:24,040
 So we see this familiar slide.

79
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:29,880
 We see the layers, the different layers of an elastic artery, particularly.

80
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:31,360
 This could be the aorta.

81
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:32,640
 This is the aorta actually.

82
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:34,400
 So we have that centralized lumen.

83
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:42,280
 We have that innermost layer which is the tunica intima and then this very thick middle muscular.

84
00:06:42,280 --> 00:06:46,880
 This is the tunica media with a high elastin content.

85
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:52,480
 And then the outermost layer being the tunica externa made up of connective tissue also called

86
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:57,120
 the tunica adventitia.

87
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:02,120
 The next type of artery are the muscular or distributing arteries.

88
00:07:02,120 --> 00:07:06,760
 These arteries actually lie distal to the elastic arteries.

89
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:11,800
 And their diameters range from 1 centimeter to 0.3 millimeters.

90
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:16,080
 These include most named arteries.

91
00:07:16,080 --> 00:07:19,520
 Like most arteries, the tunica media is thick.

92
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:24,280
 Some unique features about the muscular or distributing arteries.

93
00:07:24,280 --> 00:07:28,080
 They have an internal and external elastic laminae.

94
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:36,520
 So the internal elastic laminae forms the outer layer of the tunica intima while the external

95
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:45,400
 elastic membrane or external elastic laminae forms the outer layer of the tunica media.

96
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:52,600
 Again, these elastic membranes in addition to the elastin found within the tunica media

97
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:57,960
 will help to dampen that pulsatile pressure produced by the heartbeat.

98
00:07:57,960 --> 00:08:03,880
 And on this next slide, we can see an example of muscular or distributing artery.

99
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:12,720
 Again, we have the lumen and we have the tunica intima and that tunica intima has an outer

100
00:08:12,720 --> 00:08:15,920
 layer of the internal elastic membrane.

101
00:08:15,920 --> 00:08:23,920
 We have this thick tunica media that contains the elastin fibers also that middle muscular

102
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:27,080
 layer and external to that layer.

103
00:08:27,080 --> 00:08:29,680
 We have an external elastic membrane.

104
00:08:29,680 --> 00:08:38,960
 And here we see the outermost layer that tunica, externa, or tunica and ventisha.

105
00:08:38,960 --> 00:08:42,080
 Arterials are the smallest of the arteries.

106
00:08:42,080 --> 00:08:48,680
 Their diameters range from 0.3 millimeters to 10 micrometers.

107
00:08:48,680 --> 00:08:54,240
 The larger arterials possess all three layers or all three tunics.

108
00:08:54,240 --> 00:09:00,360
 And the diameter of arterials is controlled by local factors in the tissues, as well as

109
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:06,320
 the sympathetic nervous system that fight or flight response.

110
00:09:06,320 --> 00:09:13,640
 And here we see small arterials, some containing all three layers, usually the larger ones contain

111
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:14,640
 all three layers.

112
00:09:14,640 --> 00:09:15,640
 So we can see the lumen.

113
00:09:15,640 --> 00:09:21,480
 We see that inner tunica intima made up of endothelium, and we see that middle layer,

114
00:09:21,480 --> 00:09:26,640
 the tunica media, and then the outermost layer, the tunica externa.

115
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:31,800
 Now, capillaries are the smallest blood vessels.

116
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:38,040
 Their diameter ranges anywhere from 8 to 10 micrometers or microns, and usually just

117
00:09:38,040 --> 00:09:43,600
 a large enough for red blood cells to pass through in a single file.

118
00:09:43,600 --> 00:09:47,840
 Now we have slight specific functions of capillaries.

119
00:09:47,840 --> 00:09:53,440
 For example, capillary network in the lungs, this is where oxygen enters blood.

120
00:09:53,440 --> 00:09:59,640
 We have diffusion exchange of gases, so oxygen will enter the blood, whereas carbon dioxide

121
00:09:59,640 --> 00:10:04,000
 will leave the blood so that it can be exhaled out by the lungs.

122
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:10,400
 Within the small intestines, capillaries are important because they receive digestive nutrients

123
00:10:10,400 --> 00:10:18,120
 when we break down our food into smaller molecules, these capillaries that will receive the smaller

124
00:10:18,120 --> 00:10:21,960
 molecules to be transported to our tissues.

125
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:25,960
 Within the endocrine glands, our capillaries help pick up hormones that are secreted by

126
00:10:25,960 --> 00:10:26,960
 endocrine glands.

127
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:33,920
 In the kidneys, the capillaries are responsible for removal of nitrogynous waste products

128
00:10:33,920 --> 00:10:38,520
 formed by the kidneys.

129
00:10:38,520 --> 00:10:44,400
 And here we see a capillary whose diameter is just large enough for red blood cells to

130
00:10:44,400 --> 00:10:50,880
 kind of pass through in a single file.

131
00:10:50,880 --> 00:10:52,640
 We have different types of capillaries.

132
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:54,200
 We have continuous capillaries.

133
00:10:54,200 --> 00:10:59,160
 This is the most common type of capillary that occurs in most organs.

134
00:10:59,160 --> 00:11:05,440
 We have tight junctions and desmosomes that help join epithelial cells.

135
00:11:05,440 --> 00:11:10,720
 We also have intercellular claps, and these are gaps of unjoined membranes that allow

136
00:11:10,720 --> 00:11:15,200
 small molecules in and out of capillaries.

137
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:19,840
 So here we see a continuous capillary, and it's the least permeable of the capillaries,

138
00:11:19,840 --> 00:11:21,760
 but is the most common.

139
00:11:21,760 --> 00:11:27,480
 So here we see the different structures that make up the continuous capillary.

140
00:11:27,480 --> 00:11:33,960
 We have the intercellular claps between the endothelial cells.

141
00:11:33,960 --> 00:11:37,240
 We have tight junctions between these cells.

142
00:11:37,240 --> 00:11:45,280
 And we can see within the lumen of the capillary red blood cell.

143
00:11:45,280 --> 00:11:50,440
 Finestrated capillaries, these are capillaries that are joined by tight junctions and desmosomes.

144
00:11:50,440 --> 00:11:57,800
 They have pores in their endotheliums, pores to allow molecules to pass through, or gases

145
00:11:57,800 --> 00:11:59,160
 to pass through.

146
00:11:59,160 --> 00:12:05,560
 So fenestrated capillaries occur where high rates of exchange occur, for example, in the

147
00:12:05,560 --> 00:12:09,840
 intestines with the exchange of digested nutrients.

148
00:12:09,840 --> 00:12:19,720
 The glomeruli of kidneys where we have capillaries that help receive nitrogenous waste products

149
00:12:19,720 --> 00:12:28,440
 and then endocrine glands where we have secretion of hormones.

150
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:30,640
 So here we see a fenestrated capillary.

151
00:12:30,640 --> 00:12:35,680
 Again, we have a large fenestration, also known as pores.

152
00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:43,640
 We can see these pores between the endothelial cells to help increase permeability of molecules

153
00:12:43,640 --> 00:12:45,040
 or gases.

154
00:12:45,040 --> 00:12:52,400
 So occurs in areas of active absorption or filtration such as the kidney and small intestine.

155
00:12:52,400 --> 00:12:57,640
 Now sinusoids are wide leaky capillaries found in some organs that are usually porous

156
00:12:57,640 --> 00:12:59,200
 or fenestrated.

157
00:12:59,200 --> 00:13:05,080
 The intracellular clefs are wide open and these occur in bone marrow and spleen.

158
00:13:05,080 --> 00:13:07,320
 We heard about sinusoids in the spleen before.

159
00:13:07,320 --> 00:13:12,560
 So sinusoids have a large diameter and a twisted course.

160
00:13:12,560 --> 00:13:19,640
 So here we see large endothelial clefs or intracellular clasps between the endothelial

161
00:13:19,640 --> 00:13:26,200
 cells and again they are joined by tight junctions and we can see that there is a bit of an incomplete

162
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:27,840
 basement membrane.

163
00:13:27,840 --> 00:13:34,840
 So very large and porous and these are the most permeable and occurs in special locations

164
00:13:34,840 --> 00:13:41,280
 such as the liver, the bone marrow and the spleen.

165
00:13:41,280 --> 00:13:46,760
 So with regards to permeability, there are four roots into and out of capillaries.

166
00:13:46,760 --> 00:13:50,680
 We have direct diffusion of molecules.

167
00:13:50,680 --> 00:13:57,640
 We also have roots through the intracellular clefs, those spaces between the endothelial

168
00:13:57,640 --> 00:13:58,640
 cells.

169
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:09,120
 We also have permeability through cytoplasmic vesicles and then through pores or fenestrations.

170
00:14:09,120 --> 00:14:15,080
 Now we have areas that have capillaries that are of low permeability, specifically the

171
00:14:15,080 --> 00:14:21,200
 blood-brain barrier, within the blood-brain barrier, capillaries have complete tight junctions

172
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:24,640
 and there are no intracellular clefs present.

173
00:14:24,640 --> 00:14:31,360
 And only the vital molecules can pass through so it's a very highly selective transport mechanism.

174
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:38,360
 However, does not provide a barrier against oxygen, carbon dioxide, which is important

175
00:14:38,360 --> 00:14:45,560
 for gas exchange and some anesthetics, so some anesthesia that provide that numb feeling

176
00:14:45,560 --> 00:14:51,480
 so that we can perform surgery or some sort of procedure without harming our patient or

177
00:14:51,480 --> 00:14:56,120
 causing our patient pain.

178
00:14:56,120 --> 00:15:00,160
 Capillary beds are a network of capillaries running through tissues.

179
00:15:00,160 --> 00:15:05,360
 We have structures called pre-capillary sphincters that help regulate the flow of blood into the

180
00:15:05,360 --> 00:15:06,920
 tissues.

181
00:15:06,920 --> 00:15:13,200
 We know that structures such as tendons and ligaments are poorly vascularized and even

182
00:15:13,200 --> 00:15:18,840
 epithelium and cartilage are a vascular, meaning they don't have their own blood supply but

183
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:24,840
 instead receive nutrients from nearby connected tissue.

184
00:15:24,840 --> 00:15:29,400
 So here is an example of an anatomy bed.

185
00:15:29,400 --> 00:15:40,420
 We can see where gas exchange occurs, so the capillaries deliver oxygen to the tissues

186
00:15:40,420 --> 00:15:48,240
 and then the distal end of the capillary bed will receive carbon dioxide, will exchange

187
00:15:48,240 --> 00:15:56,480
 the carbon dioxide and then transfer the deoxygenated blood to the venules and then to the veins.

188
00:15:56,480 --> 00:16:01,600
 But again, we do have these structures called pre-capillary sphincters that kind of regulate

189
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:07,960
 the flow, the blood flow through the capillaries.

190
00:16:07,960 --> 00:16:15,320
 So here we can see a terminal arterial feeding into the capillary bed bringing oxygenated

191
00:16:15,320 --> 00:16:21,920
 blood to the capillary bed and then we have the post-capillary venule which will carry

192
00:16:21,920 --> 00:16:26,200
 deoxygenated blood away from the capillary bed.

193
00:16:26,200 --> 00:16:35,960
 So almost all tissues contain these capillary beds that again allow for the delivery of

194
00:16:35,960 --> 00:16:41,960
 oxygen to tissues and then the carrying away of waste products as well as carbon dioxide

195
00:16:41,960 --> 00:16:46,880
 away from tissues to go back towards the heart.

196
00:16:46,880 --> 00:16:55,720
 When we have relaxation of these pre-capillaries, we can see blood passing through all the capillaries

197
00:16:55,720 --> 00:16:57,160
 within this capillary bed.

198
00:16:57,160 --> 00:17:05,880
 So we have the true capillaries that allow for that gas exchange of oxygen and then carbon

199
00:17:05,880 --> 00:17:07,880
 dioxide.

200
00:17:07,880 --> 00:17:15,160
 However, when these pre-capillary sphincters close or tighten, there's only blood flow

201
00:17:15,160 --> 00:17:19,640
 that goes through this structure called the met arterial.

202
00:17:19,640 --> 00:17:26,480
 So it actually bypasses the true capillaries within the capillary bed.

203
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:32,400
 And this occurs when the tissue has lower oxygen demands, such as when nearby tissue

204
00:17:32,400 --> 00:17:39,600
 cells already have adequate oxygen, so the pre-capillary sphincters will close off the

205
00:17:39,600 --> 00:17:44,920
 true capillaries and force the blood through that met arterial again and flows straight

206
00:17:44,920 --> 00:17:55,600
 through into the thoroughfarer channel and then into the post-capillary venule.

207
00:17:55,600 --> 00:17:59,880
 So now we're going to talk about venous vessels, venous vessels.

208
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:08,280
 So veins, the definition, basically they conduct blood from the capillaries toward the heart.

209
00:18:08,280 --> 00:18:12,960
 Blood pressure is much lower in veins compared to arteries.

210
00:18:12,960 --> 00:18:19,840
 We talked about venules being the first vein that kind of receives blood from the capillary

211
00:18:19,840 --> 00:18:20,840
 bed.

212
00:18:20,840 --> 00:18:25,600
 So we have venules that are the smallest veins and their diameters can range anywhere from

213
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:29,960
 8 to 100 microns or micrometers.

214
00:18:29,960 --> 00:18:35,640
 The smallest venules we talked about are called post-capillary venules and venules will eventually

215
00:18:35,640 --> 00:18:40,040
 join to form larger veins.

216
00:18:40,040 --> 00:18:44,320
 Because we talked about having structural differences compared to arteries, there are

217
00:18:44,320 --> 00:18:50,880
 lumens, the space within the blood vessel or within the vein are a lot larger compared

218
00:18:50,880 --> 00:18:51,880
 to arteries.

219
00:18:51,880 --> 00:18:59,600
 65% of blood that we have in our bodies are contained in the veins at any given time.

220
00:18:59,600 --> 00:19:06,920
 We know that the tunica external, that outermost layer, is actually thicker compared to arteries.

221
00:19:06,920 --> 00:19:12,920
 But we also know that veins have a less elastin in their walls and these walls are thinner

222
00:19:12,920 --> 00:19:16,920
 compared to the arteries.

223
00:19:16,920 --> 00:19:24,280
 And this table in your book, Table 20.1, just basically is a summary of the anatomy of blood

224
00:19:24,280 --> 00:19:32,680
 vessels and it compares the average lumen diameter and wall thickness of the different

225
00:19:32,680 --> 00:19:42,000
 types of vessels and then it will show you the relative tissue makeup within the endothelium

226
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:48,200
 elastic tissues smooth muscles as well as fibrous collagenous tissues.

227
00:19:48,200 --> 00:19:54,480
 So I would definitely just to kind of summarize the general characteristics of the blood vessels

228
00:19:54,480 --> 00:19:58,520
 that we talked about in the previous slides use this table to study.

229
00:19:58,520 --> 00:20:04,720
 So here we see elastic artery, the largest of the arteries and then going down to the

230
00:20:04,720 --> 00:20:08,320
 muscular artery and the different characteristics of the two.

231
00:20:08,320 --> 00:20:15,920
 And then we have arterials and capillaries, then we'll have the venules, the smallest

232
00:20:15,920 --> 00:20:23,520
 of the veins, and the smallest one we talked about was post-capillary venule receiving

233
00:20:23,520 --> 00:20:28,320
 deoxygenic blood from the capillary bed, which will then feed into veins.

234
00:20:28,320 --> 00:20:34,160
 So we can see distinct differences of the tissue makeup between veins and arteries.

235
00:20:34,160 --> 00:20:42,120
 So make sure you study this table to kind of get an idea of the different characteristics.

236
00:20:42,120 --> 00:20:48,240
 Now we have specific mechanisms to help counteract low venous pressure within veins.

237
00:20:48,240 --> 00:20:52,480
 We have structures called valves and valves are not contained in all veins but they are

238
00:20:52,480 --> 00:20:56,360
 contained in some veins, particularly in the limbs.

239
00:20:56,360 --> 00:21:00,200
 Vals help prevent backflow of blood.

240
00:21:00,200 --> 00:21:04,840
 Valves are not located in the veins of the thoracic and abdominal cavities.

241
00:21:04,840 --> 00:21:07,720
 We also have the skeletal muscle pump.

242
00:21:07,720 --> 00:21:15,800
 So our muscles press against the thin walled veins to help allow for the flow of blood.

243
00:21:15,800 --> 00:21:18,880
 We can actually see it in this next slide.

244
00:21:18,880 --> 00:21:26,160
 We can see how the contraction of skeletal muscle kind of pushes the blood through our

245
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:27,680
 veins.

246
00:21:27,680 --> 00:21:38,600
 And we can see that the valves will help prevent the backflow of blood.

247
00:21:38,600 --> 00:21:44,160
 So vessels will interconnect to form vascular and nastomosis.

248
00:21:44,160 --> 00:21:49,960
 Nastomosis are just connections between these vessels.

249
00:21:49,960 --> 00:21:54,640
 Organs will receive blood from more than one arterial source.

250
00:21:54,640 --> 00:22:00,360
 We'll have neighboring arteries that form an arterial anastomosis which helps provide

251
00:22:00,360 --> 00:22:01,680
 collateral channels.

252
00:22:01,680 --> 00:22:09,680
 So if we have tissue that has a blood supply, it will have a main artery that gives its

253
00:22:09,680 --> 00:22:16,840
 blood supply, however, will have collateral arteries that also help give blood supply

254
00:22:16,840 --> 00:22:22,280
 to that tissue so that if we have one of the arteries kind of cut off, we'll have other

255
00:22:22,280 --> 00:22:30,440
 arteries also kind of making up for that cut off artery to provide additional blood supply

256
00:22:30,440 --> 00:22:32,520
 to that tissue.

257
00:22:32,520 --> 00:22:39,640
 So provide collateral channels is one of the characteristics of arterial anastomosis.

258
00:22:39,640 --> 00:22:47,080
 Veins will anastomosis more frequently than arteries.

259
00:22:47,080 --> 00:22:50,800
 So we talked about the pulmonary circulation.

260
00:22:50,800 --> 00:22:56,280
 The first great vessel that we talked about is the pulmonary trunk.

261
00:22:56,280 --> 00:23:02,880
 You know the pulmonary trunk is that a large vessel that will leave the right ventricle

262
00:23:02,880 --> 00:23:07,520
 and the blood has to pass through the pulmonary semilunar valves.

263
00:23:07,520 --> 00:23:11,480
 The pulmonary trunk will divide into right and left pulmonary arteries.

264
00:23:11,480 --> 00:23:17,360
 Again, these pulmonary arteries are the only arteries in the body that transport deoxygenated

265
00:23:17,360 --> 00:23:21,480
 blood or oxygen poor blood.

266
00:23:21,480 --> 00:23:28,840
 It still fits the definition of an artery in which these are vessels that carry blood

267
00:23:28,840 --> 00:23:31,080
 away from the heart.

268
00:23:31,080 --> 00:23:39,680
 So from the pulmonary arteries, blood will be transported to the lungs where it will

269
00:23:39,680 --> 00:23:45,760
 get reoxygenated, will have a gas exchange occurring at the capillary level, and then

270
00:23:45,760 --> 00:23:51,840
 the reoxygenated blood will be transported to the superior and inferior pulmonary veins

271
00:23:51,840 --> 00:23:54,120
 on the right and left side.

272
00:23:54,120 --> 00:23:58,760
 These structures, these vessels will carry oxygen and blood back to the heart via the

273
00:23:58,760 --> 00:24:01,680
 left atrium.

274
00:24:01,680 --> 00:24:09,720
 The vessels of the pulmonary circuit, they have thinner walls compared to systemic vessels,

275
00:24:09,720 --> 00:24:13,600
 and the max arterial pressure is lower in the pulmonary circuit.

276
00:24:13,600 --> 00:24:18,640
 So they have thinner walls to allow for maximum gas exchange, and we can actually see that

277
00:24:18,640 --> 00:24:23,960
 in this next slide, and we'll talk more about this when we get to the respiratory system.

278
00:24:23,960 --> 00:24:30,240
 So we see that the smallest structures within the lungs are abioli.

279
00:24:30,240 --> 00:24:36,240
 These are sort of like sac structures that look like kind of grape clusters, and they're

280
00:24:36,240 --> 00:24:44,960
 very thin mold, and we can see gas exchange occurring between the abioli and the pulmonary

281
00:24:44,960 --> 00:24:45,960
 capillary.

282
00:24:45,960 --> 00:24:49,360
 So carbon dioxide is exchanged for oxygen.

283
00:24:49,360 --> 00:24:55,320
 Carbon dioxide will travel back into the abioli, whereas oxygen will travel into the pulmonary

284
00:24:55,320 --> 00:24:56,320
 capillary.

285
00:24:56,320 --> 00:25:05,760
 Again, this gas exchange allows for the exhalation of carbon dioxide and the inhalation of oxygen

286
00:25:05,760 --> 00:25:12,280
 to be transported to the tissues of the body, the tissues and organs of our body.

287
00:25:12,280 --> 00:25:18,120
 Now with the systemic circulation, again, we have systemic arteries that will carry oxygenated

288
00:25:18,120 --> 00:25:20,120
 blood away from the heart.

289
00:25:20,120 --> 00:25:27,840
 We know that the aorta is the largest artery in the body, and the aorta receives blood

290
00:25:27,840 --> 00:25:32,760
 from the left ventricle.

291
00:25:32,760 --> 00:25:40,080
 So here we see a diagram of the major arteries of the systemic circulation.

292
00:25:40,080 --> 00:25:47,640
 We can see that from the heart, blood will be pumped from the left ventricle to the aorta

293
00:25:47,640 --> 00:25:50,880
 to go to the rest of the body.

294
00:25:50,880 --> 00:25:57,680
 The branches of the aortic arch, giving blood supply to the head, neck and the upper limbs,

295
00:25:57,680 --> 00:26:04,080
 and then the descending aorta or the thoracic aorta given blood supply to the thoracic structures.

296
00:26:04,080 --> 00:26:08,920
 And then once it passes through that diaphragm, that muscle that separates the thorax from

297
00:26:08,920 --> 00:26:14,280
 the abdomen, it becomes the abdominal aorta, which will then give blood supply to organs

298
00:26:14,280 --> 00:26:21,000
 with the abdomen, and then bifurcate into the iliac arteries, the common iliac arteries

299
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:26,680
 right and left, giving blood supply to the structures within the pelvis.

300
00:26:26,680 --> 00:26:32,600
 And then once it passes that inguina canal, it becomes the femoral artery, all the way

301
00:26:32,600 --> 00:26:38,160
 down to the tibular and fibular arteries as well as the arteries within the foot, so giving

302
00:26:38,160 --> 00:26:40,760
 blood supply to the lower limbs.

303
00:26:40,760 --> 00:26:47,360
 That's the basic general idea, and we're going to break these down, break the arteries and

304
00:26:47,360 --> 00:26:53,320
 the veins down into regions so we can get a better sense of the specific blood supply

305
00:26:53,320 --> 00:26:57,120
 of the different parts of the body.

306
00:26:57,120 --> 00:27:02,840
 But this very small structure or a very small diagram, which you'll also find in your book,

307
00:27:02,840 --> 00:27:09,200
 are actually arteries that we use to palpate a pulse.

308
00:27:09,200 --> 00:27:13,360
 So here we'll have pulse points within our body.

309
00:27:13,360 --> 00:27:18,920
 So within our head we have that superficial temporal artery where we can get a temporal

310
00:27:18,920 --> 00:27:19,920
 pulse.

311
00:27:19,920 --> 00:27:25,040
 We have the facial artery located kind of anterior to the ear.

312
00:27:25,040 --> 00:27:29,000
 One of the most common pulse points is the common carotid artery within the neck when

313
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:31,960
 we are performing CPR.

314
00:27:31,960 --> 00:27:39,120
 We want to get a pulse within the neck and we are palpating the common carotid artery.

315
00:27:39,120 --> 00:27:42,520
 Another artery that's very important is the brachial artery.

316
00:27:42,520 --> 00:27:49,640
 When we are taking blood pressure with our sphinctmoanometers, this is the artery that

317
00:27:49,640 --> 00:27:53,680
 is occluded when we are pumping up our sphinctmoanometers.

318
00:27:53,680 --> 00:27:59,320
 And then releasing the valve to hear the blood rushing back into this artery, the brachial

319
00:27:59,320 --> 00:28:12,080
 artery to obtain a blood pressure amount, our systolic and diastolic blood pressure.

320
00:28:12,080 --> 00:28:16,920
 And then our radial artery, which we find at the base of the thumb, also important for

321
00:28:16,920 --> 00:28:20,320
 taking heart rate.

322
00:28:20,320 --> 00:28:25,720
 Another important artery is the femoral artery, which we can find in the inguinal region or

323
00:28:25,720 --> 00:28:28,480
 the top part of the thigh.

324
00:28:28,480 --> 00:28:32,240
 The pupilidial artery right behind our knee.

325
00:28:32,240 --> 00:28:34,840
 This can be palpated also to find a pulse.

326
00:28:34,840 --> 00:28:41,720
 If we have, for example, a patient that is an amputee that doesn't have any arms and

327
00:28:41,720 --> 00:28:44,600
 we want to palpate a pulse within their lower limbs.

328
00:28:44,600 --> 00:28:48,560
 We can find it in the pupilidial artery.

329
00:28:48,560 --> 00:28:53,120
 Also the posterior tibular artery is an artery that we can find a pulse.

330
00:28:53,120 --> 00:28:59,920
 And more common is the dorsalis pedis artery, this artery that we can find at the dorsum

331
00:28:59,920 --> 00:29:03,720
 of the foot or the top part of our foot.

332
00:29:03,720 --> 00:29:10,000
 So these are very important pulse points to know, especially if you are going into any

333
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:15,240
 health care related field.

334
00:29:15,240 --> 00:29:23,440
 So first we're going to start talking about the blood vessels within the thorax, specifically

335
00:29:23,440 --> 00:29:27,360
 the blood vessels coming from the heart.

336
00:29:27,360 --> 00:29:32,280
 We have the aorta, one of the largest arteries in the body.

337
00:29:32,280 --> 00:29:34,280
 We have different parts of the aorta.

338
00:29:34,280 --> 00:29:37,880
 The first part of the aorta is the ascending aorta.

339
00:29:37,880 --> 00:29:40,680
 The ascending aorta arises from the left ventricle.

340
00:29:40,680 --> 00:29:50,560
 So blood coming from the left ventricle has to go through the aortic semilunar valve to

341
00:29:50,560 --> 00:29:54,400
 be transported to this first part of the aorta, which is the ascending aorta.

342
00:29:54,400 --> 00:29:59,720
 Now the ascending aorta is important because we have branches of our coronary arteries coming

343
00:29:59,720 --> 00:30:07,600
 from this first section and the coronary arteries provide blood supply to the heart itself.

344
00:30:07,600 --> 00:30:11,120
 From the ascending aorta it becomes the aortic arch.

345
00:30:11,120 --> 00:30:16,720
 The aortic arch lies posterior to the menubrium of the sternum and has three very important

346
00:30:16,720 --> 00:30:18,720
 branches.

347
00:30:18,720 --> 00:30:22,320
 The first branch I usually like to call it the right brachiocephalic trunk.

348
00:30:22,320 --> 00:30:26,600
 The right brachiocephalic trunk because it gives blood supply to the right side of the

349
00:30:26,600 --> 00:30:29,960
 head neck as well as the right upper limb.

350
00:30:29,960 --> 00:30:33,720
 So the right brachiocephalic trunk is the first branch and eventually it will branch

351
00:30:33,720 --> 00:30:41,000
 off to form the right common carotid artery as well as the right subclavian artery.

352
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:44,960
 And again giving blood supply to the right side of the head neck and the right upper

353
00:30:44,960 --> 00:30:46,120
 limb.

354
00:30:46,120 --> 00:30:49,480
 The second branch is the left common carotid artery.

355
00:30:49,480 --> 00:30:55,120
 So the left common carotid artery would give blood supply eventually to the left side of

356
00:30:55,120 --> 00:30:56,640
 the head neck.

357
00:30:56,640 --> 00:31:01,760
 The third branch coming off of the aortic arch is the left subclavian artery.

358
00:31:01,760 --> 00:31:09,040
 The left subclavian artery will then give blood supply to the left upper limb.

359
00:31:09,040 --> 00:31:15,800
 And here is a diagram of the heart showing the aorta which is the largest artery in the

360
00:31:15,800 --> 00:31:16,800
 body.

361
00:31:16,800 --> 00:31:21,920
 And the first part being the ascending artery and we can see here the branches of the ascending

362
00:31:21,920 --> 00:31:27,680
 artery we have the right coronary artery going through that coronary sulcus and then kind

363
00:31:27,680 --> 00:31:33,240
 of going posterior to the pulmonary trunk we have the left coronary artery and its two

364
00:31:33,240 --> 00:31:42,360
 branches the left anterior descending artery and the circumflex artery here.

365
00:31:42,360 --> 00:31:47,880
 The ascending aorta will then become the aortic arch with its three branches we have the right

366
00:31:47,880 --> 00:31:57,120
 brachiocephalic trunk the left common carotid artery and the left subclavian artery.

367
00:31:57,120 --> 00:32:03,240
 The descending aorta is the section of the aorta after the aortic arch continues from

368
00:32:03,240 --> 00:32:11,560
 the aortic arch and it is also synonymous with the thoracic aorta in the thorax.

369
00:32:11,560 --> 00:32:20,160
 So the thoracic aorta is in the region of T5 to T12 so vertebrae T5 to T12.

370
00:32:20,160 --> 00:32:25,640
 Once it goes through the diaphragm again that muscle that separates the thorax from the

371
00:32:25,640 --> 00:32:29,240
 abdomen it becomes the abdominal aorta.

372
00:32:29,240 --> 00:32:33,280
 The abdominal aorta will end at L4.

373
00:32:33,280 --> 00:32:39,480
 At L4 the abdominal aorta will then divide or bifurcate into right and left common iliac

374
00:32:39,480 --> 00:32:41,040
 arteries.

375
00:32:41,040 --> 00:32:51,000
 So that is the basic structure of the aorta coming from the heart specifically the left

376
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:53,120
 ventricle of the heart.

377
00:32:53,120 --> 00:32:59,120
 We will then get into the arteries of the head neck.

378
00:32:59,120 --> 00:33:01,840
 We have pairs of arteries supplying the head neck.

379
00:33:01,840 --> 00:33:09,000
 We have the carotid arteries which are branches of the aortic arch.

380
00:33:09,000 --> 00:33:14,720
 The right common carotid artery coming from the right brachiocephalic trunk and then the

381
00:33:14,720 --> 00:33:21,800
 left common carotid artery coming from as the second branch off of the aortic arch.

382
00:33:21,800 --> 00:33:26,720
 And then we also have branches of the subclavian arteries.

383
00:33:26,720 --> 00:33:30,880
 We have the vertebral arteries that we talked about when we were going over bones.

384
00:33:30,880 --> 00:33:37,640
 These arteries go through the transverse formin within the transverse processes of the cervical

385
00:33:37,640 --> 00:33:39,520
 vertebrae.

386
00:33:39,520 --> 00:33:45,480
 We also have branches of the subclavian arteries that include the thyroservical trunk giving

387
00:33:45,480 --> 00:33:54,280
 blood supply to the neck region around the thyroid gland and the costo cervical trunk

388
00:33:54,280 --> 00:33:56,760
 which we will see in the next diagram.

389
00:33:56,760 --> 00:34:00,360
 So here are arteries of the head neck.

390
00:34:00,360 --> 00:34:03,600
 We see branches of the subclavian artery.

391
00:34:03,600 --> 00:34:09,560
 We have the vertebral artery again going through the transverse formin of the cervical vertebrae

392
00:34:09,560 --> 00:34:15,520
 which will eventually go into the base of the skull to help contribute to the circle

393
00:34:15,520 --> 00:34:16,880
 of willis.

394
00:34:16,880 --> 00:34:25,400
 We have the thyroservical trunk and the costo cervical trunk again giving blood supply to

395
00:34:25,400 --> 00:34:29,200
 the head and neck.

396
00:34:29,200 --> 00:34:35,120
 We can see that the common carotid artery will eventually branch to form the external

397
00:34:35,120 --> 00:34:45,520
 carotid artery as well as the internal carotid artery again going into that carotid canal

398
00:34:45,520 --> 00:34:50,440
 in the base of the skull to help contribute to the circle of willis which is the blood

399
00:34:50,440 --> 00:34:54,440
 supply of the brain.

400
00:34:54,440 --> 00:34:58,560
 The common carotid arteries are located in the anterior triangle of the neck.

401
00:34:58,560 --> 00:35:02,440
 The two branches of the common carotid artery that we talked about are the external carotid

402
00:35:02,440 --> 00:35:05,280
 artery as well as the internal carotid artery.

403
00:35:05,280 --> 00:35:10,200
 We have right and left common carotid arteries as well as right and left external internal

404
00:35:10,200 --> 00:35:14,480
 carotid arteries.

405
00:35:14,480 --> 00:35:20,640
 Here are the branches of the external carotid artery giving blood supply to the external

406
00:35:20,640 --> 00:35:24,680
 part of the head and neck.

407
00:35:24,680 --> 00:35:31,360
 We have the superior thyroid artery, the lingual artery, facial artery, occipital artery, posterior

408
00:35:31,360 --> 00:35:35,640
 erycular, posterior erycular behind the ear.

409
00:35:35,640 --> 00:35:41,560
 Superficial temporal artery, that's one artery that we can find at the temple of the head

410
00:35:41,560 --> 00:35:46,480
 around the frontal bone where we can palpate a pulse point.

411
00:35:46,480 --> 00:35:51,400
 Also an important artery because when we do one of those sliding thermometers it kind

412
00:35:51,400 --> 00:35:57,440
 of goes over that superficial temporal artery to give a temperature of a patient and then

413
00:35:57,440 --> 00:36:01,680
 the maxillary artery.

414
00:36:01,680 --> 00:36:11,440
 The internal carotid artery has the ophthalmic artery giving blood supply to the eye's internal

415
00:36:11,440 --> 00:36:16,320
 and anterior cerebral artery giving blood supply to the anterior part of the brain.

416
00:36:16,320 --> 00:36:21,040
 Anterior communicating artery help form the cerebral arterial circle or the circle of

417
00:36:21,040 --> 00:36:27,580
 willis and the middle cerebral artery.

418
00:36:27,580 --> 00:36:32,760
 So that's it for the carotid arteries, the subclavian arteries.

419
00:36:32,760 --> 00:36:38,000
 We know that the right subclavian artery originates from the brachiosophallic trunk.

420
00:36:38,000 --> 00:36:45,960
 The left subclavian artery is the third branch off of the aortic arch.

421
00:36:45,960 --> 00:36:52,960
 The vertebral arteries, a branch of the subclavian artery, supplies the posterior brain.

422
00:36:52,960 --> 00:36:57,120
 The vertebral arteries will join to form the basilar artery.

423
00:36:57,120 --> 00:37:06,640
 The basilar artery will then divide into two posterior cerebral arteries and then join

424
00:37:06,640 --> 00:37:11,920
 to form the posterior communicating arteries that connect the posterior cerebral arteries

425
00:37:11,920 --> 00:37:14,480
 to the middle cerebral arteries.

426
00:37:14,480 --> 00:37:21,580
 These arteries contributing to the circle of willis which we'll see in the next slide.

427
00:37:21,580 --> 00:37:24,760
 So here we see the circle of willis.

428
00:37:24,760 --> 00:37:31,000
 The circle of willis we can see the two vertebral arteries coming up from the cervical vertebrae.

429
00:37:31,000 --> 00:37:36,960
 They will join together to form this basilar artery here which will then kind of bifurcate

430
00:37:36,960 --> 00:37:41,940
 to form the posterior cerebral arteries giving blood supply to the posterior part of the

431
00:37:41,940 --> 00:37:43,240
 brain.

432
00:37:43,240 --> 00:37:47,520
 And then coming off the posterior cerebral arteries are the posterior communicating arteries

433
00:37:47,520 --> 00:37:55,560
 which will join with the internal crowded artery here and then form the anterior cerebral

434
00:37:55,560 --> 00:38:02,560
 artery here going towards the anterior part of the brain.

435
00:38:02,560 --> 00:38:07,640
 And then between the anterior cerebral artery we have an anterior communicating artery.

436
00:38:07,640 --> 00:38:14,640
 So these arteries forming that circle of willis also known as the cerebral arterial circle

437
00:38:14,640 --> 00:38:23,320
 giving blood supply to the different parts of the brain.

438
00:38:23,320 --> 00:38:29,760
 So with regards to branches of the subclavian artery we have the thyroid cervical trunk.

439
00:38:29,760 --> 00:38:36,840
 These send two branches posteriorly over the scapula sends one branch anteriorly to the

440
00:38:36,840 --> 00:38:42,400
 inferior part of the thyroid gland which is located in the anterior part of the neck.

441
00:38:42,400 --> 00:38:47,880
 The cost of cervical trunk sends a branch superiorly to the deep muscles of the neck.

442
00:38:47,880 --> 00:38:54,080
 Also will send a branch inferiorly to the intercosto spaces.

443
00:38:54,080 --> 00:39:00,320
 So that is the blood supply to the going to the head and neck.

444
00:39:00,320 --> 00:39:03,840
 Now we're going to talk about the arteries of the thorax.

445
00:39:03,840 --> 00:39:08,480
 We have the internal thoracic arteries that supply the anterior thoracic walls.

446
00:39:08,480 --> 00:39:11,320
 We have the anterior intercostal arteries.

447
00:39:11,320 --> 00:39:14,840
 So intercostal means between the ribs.

448
00:39:14,840 --> 00:39:22,480
 These are branches of the internal thoracic arteries and we have posterior intercostal arteries

449
00:39:22,480 --> 00:39:27,520
 giving blood supply to the inferior nine pairs, I'm sorry not giving blood supply but the

450
00:39:27,520 --> 00:39:36,400
 inferior nine pairs will arise from the thoracic aorta.

451
00:39:36,400 --> 00:39:41,560
 So moving on to the arteries of the upper limb.

452
00:39:41,560 --> 00:39:44,120
 The subclavian artery.

453
00:39:44,120 --> 00:39:48,840
 On the right the subclavian artery is a branch of the right brachusophilic trunk.

454
00:39:48,840 --> 00:39:55,440
 On the left the subclavian artery is a direct branch coming off of the aortic arch.

455
00:39:55,440 --> 00:39:56,440
 It's the third branch.

456
00:39:56,440 --> 00:40:01,480
 So the subclavian artery will enter the axilla as the axillary artery.

457
00:40:01,480 --> 00:40:09,080
 So a border that I like to use to kind of help you determine when the subclavian artery

458
00:40:09,080 --> 00:40:14,520
 turns into the axillary artery is the inferior border of the first rib.

459
00:40:14,520 --> 00:40:20,760
 So once the subclavian artery passes by the inferior border of the first rib it becomes

460
00:40:20,760 --> 00:40:27,000
 the axillary artery which you'll find basically in your armpit area.

461
